that I just read a book.

37
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:34,120
So well, yes, reason number 25 to buy my book.

38
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:35,880
Thank you.

39
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:41,000
This week is one of the best weeks of my year because I'm at summer camp and I'm

40
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,800
pre-recording the podcast, and it's just going to be a short visit and I'm going

41
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:53,000
to replay a story from a podcast almost two years ago and it's called The Boy

42
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:58,080
from Our Hoose and it's going to take up the rest of the podcast and we'll be back

43
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:01,960
again to talk to you next week and hopefully have some things from summer

44
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:03,440
camp to report on.

45
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:07,480
But until then, we've got The Boy from Our Hoose.

46
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:11,080
So, hey, let's get started, shall we?

47
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:21,680
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

48
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:23,520
The year is 1926.

49
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:24,800
We're in New Jersey-

50
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:34,240
Yeah, New Jersey, and we're in a warehouse and a crate, a big heavy crate of World

51
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:40,200
War I surplus semaphore flagpoles.

52
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:46,800
It sounds like a bad joke, I know, but this actually happened.

53
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:51,280
This big heavy crate shifted and fell

54
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:58,480
And because of that the whole face of worldwide scouting changed.

55
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:03,280
Now I'm a little ahead of the story, obviously.

56
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:07,720
Now I understand why that crate is so important.

57
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:13,280
We've got to go back about 26 years and then we have to go a considerable distance from

58
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:15,880
that warehouse in New Jersey.

59
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:21,279
We've got to go all the way over the Atlantic to Our Hoose, Denmark.

60
00:04:21,279 --> 00:04:29,600
In August of 1900, a boy was born in the city of Our Hoose named Wilhelm Bihara

61
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:31,880
Gard Jensen.

62
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:38,240
Wilhelm grew up and became a boy who liked to go roaming through the woods outside Our

63
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:39,640
Hoose.

64
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:44,920
His older brother, Harold, sent him a Christmas present one year, and that Christmas present

65
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:48,520
was the book Baden Powell's Scouting for Boys.

66
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:53,760
The book had been a big smash success all over Europe and had been translated into

67
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:54,760
Danish.

68
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:57,880
Well, Wil read the book and discovered that scouts did the kind of things that he

69
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:02,719
wanted to do when he was out there in the woods hiking and tracking and camping and

70
00:05:02,719 --> 00:05:06,520
cooking over fires and all this great stuff.

71
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:11,719
And he also discovered that to become a boy scout you had to join a patrol.

72
00:05:11,719 --> 00:05:15,880
Well, there was no patrol around- not that Wil knew about-

73
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:19,600
So he got his buddies together and he made up a patrol.

74
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:24,840
That patrol did the sort of adventurous stuff that he had hoped they would do.

75
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:30,080
They got out in the woods outside of Our Hoose and went camping and hiking,

76
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:35,200
But they had meetings that kind of devolved into you know, some kind of rough tumble games

77
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:39,000
that Wil didn't particularly care for.

78
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,360
And besides that, about 1912 there was a big downturn in the Danish economy and Wil's

79
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:50,160
family needed to move and follow his father's work from place to place for several years.

80
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:54,040
Four years later Wil and his family were back in Our Hoose.

81
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:56,720
Wil was a bit older and he formed his patrol.

82
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:58,600
He was their patrol leader

83
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,280
And they just went scouting all the time.

84
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:07,720
They camped on an island on a nearby lake and they put together theatrical presentations

85
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:10,400
with other patrols to raise some money.

86
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:11,960
They tramped around the countryside,

87
00:06:11,960 --> 00:06:16,720
They hiked and camped one summer all along the west coast of Denmark

88
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:18,440
And Wil just flourished.

89
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:26,360
He attained the rank of night scout, which was the highest in Danish scouting in 1917.

90
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:27,880
His circle of influence grew.

91
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:33,320
His patrol was now part of a real troop and he was the most senior patrol leaders and

92
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:36,440
often led the group as a whole.

93
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:40,960
He came into contact with other scout troops in the community and began to see the wider

94
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:45,520
circle of scouting as it formed in those early days.

95
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:49,880
By the time he was 20 years old, Wil was still involved with scouts who was helping lead his

96
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:54,400
troop and he was also studying to become a pharmacist.

97
00:06:54,400 --> 00:07:01,640
But then he was selected to go to the First World Jamboree at Olympia in London

98
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:04,880
And that's where he first met Baden Powell.

99
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:08,520
Going to that First World Jamboree really spiked Wil's interest.

100
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:12,640
He began to get very serious about his leadership in scouting.

101
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:19,240
He became the scoutmaster of the Troupenahus and a trainer in national training courses.

102
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:25,120
He began to write for the National Danish Scout Journal and then he actually became its editor.

103
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:26,520
His interest in journalism grew.

104
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:31,080
He also wrote for newspapers and he published a book when he was 23 years old.

105
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:32,400
It was a novel.

106
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:36,960
It was called The Island and it was based on his experience with his patrol.

107
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:44,160
At the age of 25, Wil decided that he needed to see more of the world.

108
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:47,600
His interest was piqued by going to the World Jamboree.

109
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:51,840
He decided what he was going to do was travel around the world and kind of investigate

110
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:56,560
scouting in other countries and bring the best ideas back to Denmark.

111
00:07:56,560 --> 00:08:00,560
So he started out in the United States and that year when he traveled to the United

112
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:03,400
States he got a job in a scout camp.

113
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:10,080
And after the scout camp season was over he managed to wrangle himself a job, a very menial

114
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:13,880
job, in the offices of the Boy Scouts of America.

115
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:20,520
And that's where Wil came into contact with that famous crate of World War I surplus

116
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:22,520
semaphore flagpoles,

117
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:25,800
I mean, come on.

118
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:30,960
So he's moving the crate around, the crate slips and falls and it traps his leg, his

119
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:32,600
right leg,

120
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:37,159
And it breaks his right leg. and so Wil is now in a foreign country somewhere, but he's

121
00:08:37,159 --> 00:08:38,360
being well taken care of.

122
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:43,560
He's got a busted leg. he ends up for laid up for a week in the hospital.

123
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:47,040
But you know, pretty soon he's on crutches and he's moving around.

124
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:50,280
So once Wil was out of the hospital he was back at the offices and he had a

125
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:54,560
chance encounter with the chief scout executive, James West.

126
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:58,880
Now you know the BSA was a relatively small operation at the time and West

127
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:05,960
knew who Wil was, at least by an accident report, and saw him on the crutches

128
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:07,960
there and he said: hey, how you doing?

129
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:08,960
Oh, I'm doing fine.

130
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:12,040
What do you think of the Boy Scouts of America?

131
00:09:12,040 --> 00:09:18,680
And Wil gave him a brief answer right there in person.

132
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:23,920
But he also decided that he really wanted to carefully answer West's question

133
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:26,160
because Wil was a serious man

134
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:30,480
And he had made this work the aim of his life.

135
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:38,040
So he sat down and he composed an 18 page single spaced memo and kind of dissected

136
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:40,000
the BSA's strengths and weaknesses.

137
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,959
He had a lot of good things to say about American scouting but he wasn't

138
00:09:43,959 --> 00:09:48,600
too sure that there wasn't this kind of mania for collecting merit badges.

139
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:51,439
He thought the camps were a little soft,

140
00:09:51,439 --> 00:09:54,079
They were a little luxurious.

141
00:09:54,120 --> 00:10:00,960
Most important, Wil said troops weren't really using the patrol method

142
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:03,080
that Baden Powell had set up.

143
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:07,400
Well, West read the memo and discovered that he had a very articulate,

144
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:16,760
well-studied young man working for him in a warehouse who really had a handle

145
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:19,400
on some things about scouting.

146
00:10:19,439 --> 00:10:25,880
So West persuaded him to become part of the BSA's editorial service.

147
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:27,720
One thing led to another.

148
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:32,400
One of Wil's suggestions had been that there really needed to be some kind

149
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:37,720
of a guide for patrol leaders if the patrol method was really to be applied,

150
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:40,720
And he was pretty insistent that it should be written by somebody

151
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:43,800
who had been a patrol leader or been a scout master.

152
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:48,880
And so West kind of called his bluff on that and said: well, why don't you write it?

153
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:51,000
Well, that was all that Wil needed to hear,

154
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,320
And he started working hard putting things together.

155
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:58,480
Now I'm still calling him Wil, but by that time he was calling himself Bill,

156
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:02,120
And the reason that he did that, he kind of Americanized his name.

157
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:06,439
He had Americanized his name and he changed from Wilhelm

158
00:11:06,439 --> 00:11:09,800
Behergaard Jensen to Wilhelm Behergaard.

159
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:13,280
But he noticed that people at the BSA office had a little trouble with

160
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,120
Behergaard and it was coming a little bit out like Beergarden

161
00:11:17,240 --> 00:11:19,560
And he didn't like the association.

162
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:24,920
So he freely translated his Danish name, Beher, which means hill,

163
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:29,240
and guard, which means court, to Hillcourt

164
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:34,640
And Wilhelm and William. that worked,

165
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:42,440
So he became known as William Hillcourt and to his friends, Bill.

166
00:11:42,440 --> 00:11:45,600
So now you know who Wil is right:

167
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:52,800
William Greenbar, Bill Hillcourt, the author of the first handbook for patrol leaders,

168
00:11:53,920 --> 00:12:00,440
And it was published in 1929 and translated into five other languages for

169
00:12:00,440 --> 00:12:01,920
use around the world.

170
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:06,040
While he was working at the BSA offices, Bill met his wife, Grace Brown,

171
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:10,920
who worked on the chief executive staff, and Grace was from Yonkers,

172
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:18,640
And in June of 1933, Grace Brown became Grace Hillcourt.

173
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:23,760
After a long honeymoon to Europe and tracking around and

174
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:27,280
cycling through Europe, they returned back home and

175
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:30,400
Bill began writing and editing Scouting Magazine.

176
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:33,000
Once the handbook for patrol leaders was published,

177
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:38,160
Bill was pulled in on another job and that was aimed at saving Boys Life Magazine.

178
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:39,800
It was the midst of the depression.

179
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:42,040
Boys Life was not doing well

180
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:47,280
And the Rockefeller Foundation made a small loan to help keep Boys Life afloat.

181
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:54,720
And Bill's contribution was a single page in each month's magazine aimed at patrol

182
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:59,520
leaders and the members of their patrol on all the great stuff patrols could do

183
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:02,080
And how they could advance together through Scouting,

184
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:04,240
all the great activities they could do.

185
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:09,000
And so the boy who had been a patrol leader in Ahus

186
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:15,280
now became America's patrol leader and Greenbar Bill was born.

187
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:19,640
In the years that followed, Bill's role expanded from being the BSA's

188
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:23,400
chief journalist on Scoutcraft and troop leadership to becoming one of

189
00:13:23,400 --> 00:13:26,640
the primary program researchers and developers,

190
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:31,400
And he did this in a really interesting way. a very hands-on kind of guy.

191
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:36,320
He found a perfect location: the village of Mendham, New Jersey,

192
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:41,080
just outside of the newly donated Schiff Scout Reservation.

193
00:13:41,080 --> 00:13:46,560
Bill and Grace talked Dr West into allowing them to convert an old stone

194
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:49,160
sheep barn into a house for the two of them

195
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:54,200
where they could be close to the troop that he was the scout master for

196
00:13:54,200 --> 00:14:00,040
Mendham, New Jersey's troop one was the experimental troop of the BSA

197
00:14:00,040 --> 00:14:06,280
In 1935, while they were there at Schiff Scout Reservation Bill and

198
00:14:06,280 --> 00:14:09,920
Grace met Olive and Robert.

199
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:11,839
You know who Olive and Robert are right,

200
00:14:11,839 --> 00:14:13,319
Baden Powell.

201
00:14:13,319 --> 00:14:16,640
That's a familiar name in scouting, the founder of scouting.

202
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:21,959
Lord and Lady Baden Powell were on a visit to the United States

203
00:14:21,959 --> 00:14:26,000
And they were happy to meet with people at Schiff Scout Reservation

204
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:27,720
and the training center there

205
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:31,199
And they became a particularly fond of Bill and Grace

206
00:14:31,199 --> 00:14:33,640
And it started a lifelong friendship

207
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:38,360
with Lord and Lady Baden Powell and continued with their children

208
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:39,640
and grandchildren.

209
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:43,160
Bill became one of the most familiar faces and names

210
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:45,080
in scouting in the United States.

211
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:47,760
He wrote articles for Scouting Magazine.

212
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:50,080
He wrote articles for Boys' Life.

213
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:53,199
He wrote two editions of the Handbook for Scoutmasters,

214
00:14:53,199 --> 00:14:55,439
two editions of the Boy Scout Handbook.

215
00:14:55,439 --> 00:15:00,240
He wrote the first Scout Fieldbook which just

216
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:04,840
became a classic text on basic campcraft and nature lore.

217
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:08,000
He took wood badge training under John Skinner Wilson.

218
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,720
He was the camp chief of Gilwell Park in 1936.

219
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:15,040
And after World War II Bill was instrumental in bringing

220
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:16,960
wood badge courses to the United States

221
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:19,720
And he became the first Gilwell deputy camp chief

222
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:23,600
in the United States in 1948 and the first BSA

223
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:25,480
wood badge scout master.

224
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:28,640
He edited Baden Powell's Scouting for Boys

225
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:32,080
and aids to scout master's ship to be used to help revitalize

226
00:15:32,080 --> 00:15:34,280
scouting in war-torn countries,

227
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:38,000
And the books have been translated into more than 20 languages.

228
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,080
He helped develop junior leader training courses

229
00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:43,560
and new requirements for Boy Scout advancement.

230
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:45,680
He corresponded with scouters in other countries,

231
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:46,920
devising them on training.

232
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:49,080
He met scouters from around the nation

233
00:15:49,080 --> 00:15:51,880
and the world at national and world jamborees

234
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:55,080
in which he was always in attendance.

235
00:15:55,200 --> 00:15:59,280
Bill retired from professional service with the BSA in 1965.

236
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:02,200
And he was able to put into print something

237
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:07,000
that he'd been working on for a long time with Grace

238
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:08,640
and Lady Baden Powell,

239
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:10,960
And that was the first authoritative biography

240
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:14,120
of Baden Powell published under the title Baden Powell:

241
00:16:14,120 --> 00:16:15,800
Two Lives of a Hero.

242
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:18,160
It was translated into seven foreign languages

243
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:20,320
and became the standard reference work

244
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:23,160
on Baden Powell for many, many years.

245
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:25,640
Bill and Grace traveled widely.

246
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:28,199
They visited scout friends and helped

247
00:16:28,199 --> 00:16:31,319
to implement training programs for scout associations

248
00:16:31,319 --> 00:16:32,480
all over the world.

249
00:16:32,480 --> 00:16:35,920
And Grace died in 1973,

250
00:16:35,920 --> 00:16:39,319
leaving Bill bereft of his great friend and companion.

251
00:16:39,319 --> 00:16:43,400
But he pressed on with his volunteer work and scouting.

252
00:16:43,400 --> 00:16:46,719
In the 1970s the BSA made some changes.

253
00:16:46,719 --> 00:16:49,439
Bill didn't like those changes at all.

254
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:53,320
He watched the BSA de-emphasize the outdoor aspects

255
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:56,720
of scouting and try and make some pretty desperate moves

256
00:16:56,720 --> 00:16:58,520
to remain relevant.

257
00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:02,920
But in 1978, Bill convinced chief scout executive

258
00:17:02,920 --> 00:17:07,920
Harvey Price to allow him to write a new Boy Scout handbook

259
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:10,080
and bring back the outdoor orientation

260
00:17:10,080 --> 00:17:12,160
to the Boy Scouts of America.

261
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:15,040
And Bill did this for free

262
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:18,720
And he spent a year and brought out the ninth edition

263
00:17:19,160 --> 00:17:21,440
entitled The Official Boy Scouts Handbook,

264
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:22,839
and it was an immediate success

265
00:17:22,839 --> 00:17:27,839
and it really did serve to revitalize scouting again. again.

266
00:17:27,880 --> 00:17:32,680
This was his second go round at the age of 78.

267
00:17:32,680 --> 00:17:35,160
And his efforts were recognized by the National Court

268
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:37,520
of Honor and he was awarded the silver buffalo

269
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:40,680
for distinguished service to American youth.

270
00:17:40,680 --> 00:17:43,320
Bill spent his last years as a worldwide

271
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:45,320
goodwill ambassador for scouting

272
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:48,960
And he tried to never miss a jamboree or big encampment.

273
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:52,120
And even though it had been some time

274
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:55,080
since he had written a column for Boy's Life Magazine,

275
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:59,000
his signature was still highly sought after

276
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,679
And that signature was kind of interesting.

277
00:18:01,679 --> 00:18:05,080
It was his famous Bill signature

278
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:07,840
superimposed on two green bars

279
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:11,720
At the 1989 National Jamboree.

280
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:16,720
people lined up for hours and Bill signed 7,423 autographs

281
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:22,280
in the middle of a hot Virginia summer at the age of 89..

282
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:27,640
One of those 7,423 autographs is right here

283
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:29,160
while I'm speaking to you

284
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:33,040
And it's in a copy of the original fieldbook

285
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:35,360
that Bill wrote that I happen to have

286
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:38,560
and I brought to the jamboree with me. and I stood in line

287
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:41,480
and I got to meet the man for just a moment.

288
00:18:41,520 --> 00:18:46,280
He was sitting at a table there signing books very happily

289
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:48,960
And when he saw that original copy

290
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:53,440
of the fieldbook come across he looked at it

291
00:18:53,440 --> 00:18:56,320
and he looked up at me and he looked at his assistant

292
00:18:56,320 --> 00:18:59,000
and he said, well, that one's a two-fer

293
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:03,120
And what he meant when he said that was

294
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:04,720
that he would put both signatures.

295
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:06,880
He would put the Bill signature with the green bars

296
00:19:06,880 --> 00:19:09,000
and his more formal signature in it.

297
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:11,840
And I have it and it's a treasured little artifact

298
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:15,320
that I have today from that very brief meeting

299
00:19:15,320 --> 00:19:18,360
with really one of Scouting's heroes.

300
00:19:18,360 --> 00:19:23,360
It all started with that big crate of World War I surplus

301
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:28,200
7,4 flagpoles and it led to something

302
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:32,320
that changed Scouting permanently

303
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:36,080
and to something that we've still benefit from today:

304
00:19:36,120 --> 00:19:39,120
The generous, generous heart of a great man,

305
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:42,080
William Greenbar Bill Hillcourt.

306
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:45,080
He passed away on November 9 of 1992

307
00:19:45,080 --> 00:19:47,280
while he was on a Scouting tour.

308
00:19:47,280 --> 00:19:48,600
He was in Sweden.

309
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:50,120
He was very close to his home

310
00:19:50,120 --> 00:19:52,720
and his boyhood home in Denmark,

311
00:19:52,720 --> 00:19:56,800
But he passed away there and was brought back to New Jersey

312
00:19:56,800 --> 00:19:58,800
and has entered next to his beloved grace

313
00:19:58,800 --> 00:20:02,080
there in Cemetery in New Jersey

314
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:04,360
And somebody who's well remembered.

315
00:20:04,360 --> 00:20:09,120
Now, if you haven't read the original Patrol Eater's Handbook,

316
00:20:09,120 --> 00:20:12,000
if you haven't read the old field book,

317
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:13,880
the first edition of the field book,

318
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:15,880
you know it's worth getting a copy,

319
00:20:15,880 --> 00:20:16,920
It's worth looking at

320
00:20:16,920 --> 00:20:19,800
And you can virtually shake hands

321
00:20:19,800 --> 00:20:22,280
with Greenbar Bill Hillcourt.

